[
  "[SCENE: Cam on desk]\nIn October 2026 I walked out under 345,000-volt transmission lines in Northern Illinois\nwith a smartphone to record some thoughts on software craftsmanship.\nWhen I got back to my desk and checked the footage, the audio was destroyed.\n60 Hz electromagnetic hum. Corona arcing. And what looked like a three-and-a-half-minute\nclock drift between audio and video.\n[FREEFORM: Describe what it felt like sitting there staring at the waveform - the instinct to just trash it and reshoot]\nThe standard advice is: trash it, reshoot.\nInstead, I opened a browser tab and started asking AI models what to do.\nThat's how this series started.\n[PAUSE]\nThis is The Sound Above: Minus Zero. I'm Mike Hall.\nThe bottleneck in engineering is never generation. It's verification.\n",
  "[SCENE: Terminal - show the original prompt or a browser tab with the chat]\nI started with thirteen words in a browser. Then brought it into Gemini Flash via the terminal.\nThe model immediately went to work.\nCascaded Butterworth filters. Exact duration ratios. Shell scripts. DSP pipelines.\nIt told me with total, unblinking confidence that the audio had drifted by twenty percent.\natempo=0.837. Here's the command. Run it.\n[SHOW: The actual bad atempo command the model produced]\nAnd it was completely wrong.\n[FREEFORM: Talk about the sticker on the 2009 Dell Studio XPS from the Obtiva days: \"Typing is not the bottleneck.\"\nTalk about what that meant then vs what it means now - generating code is not the bottleneck, domain understanding is]\nIn Hollywood, they yell \"Enhance!\" and pixels appear from nowhere.\nIn software, that wishful thinking produces AI slop.\n[PAUSE: let that sit]\nThe bottleneck is domain understanding. Same as it was in 2009.\n",
  "[SCENE: Show waveform or terminal output]\nThe container said: 21 minutes of video. 18 minutes of audio.\nThe model assumed linear drift and handed me atempo=0.837.\n[SHOW: Audio waveform slurred - before fix]\nWhen I tested it in headphones, the speech was slurred and completely out of sync.\n[FREEFORM: Talk about what going back to ground truth feels like - the discipline of not trusting the answer just because it came out confidently]\nSo we went back to ground truth.\nHigh-FPS frame extraction. Whisper subtitle alignment. Audio waveforms.\nWhat had actually happened?\nAt 18 minutes I said \"Thank you\" and wrapped the talk.\nThe remaining three and a half minutes was me walking in silence with the camera still running.\nThe speech was at 1.0x the whole time.\n[PAUSE]\nWhen AI models project effortless competence, how do you find the boundary?\nYou verify with empirical tests. Not conversational trust.\n",
  "[SCENE: Show phone diagram or dual-mic diagram slide]\n[SHOW: Diagram of iPhone dual-mic placement - top mic vs bottom mic]\nHere's what was actually going on with the audio.\nModern smartphones use dual mics for noise suppression.\nThe mic at the top was acting as an unshielded inductive loop antenna.\nPicking up 65 times more electromagnetic interference from the overhead towers\nthan the bottom mic.\n[SHOW: Spectral waterfall - before and after isolating bottom mic]\nIsolating the bottom mic gave us clean speech buried under the noise.\n[FREEFORM: Talk about the decision to go back out under those same wires.\nWhat you wanted to prove - was the dehum filter a one-time lucky fix or a real tool?]\nAnd I didn't leave it there as a single lucky fix.\nI walked back out under those same 345kV conductors.\nDeliberately re-attempted the scenario. Same trail, same conductors.\nI wanted to know: is this filter real, or was it one good day?\n[SHOW: Second field recording footage if cued, or just the trail photo]\n",
  "[SCENE: Terminal - show the deterministic filter running]\n[DEMO: Run the dehum filter against the second field recording - show timing output]\nThat field re-test gets to the heart of the cautionary tale.\nWhen you prompt a frontier model outside your domain, it hands you math and code\nwith total, fluent confidence. You look like an audio engineering expert.\nBut you're throwing a ball high into the air.\nIf you throw it too high, far beyond what you can verify and catch,\nit comes straight down on your head.\n[SHOW: just3ws.localhost/dehum/dashboard.html - the A/B listening comparison]\n[FREEFORM: Walk through what the dashboard shows - the raw audio, the hum-removed audio,\nthe spectral diff, the filter parameters that survived the second test]\nWhen we ran the re-attempt footage through the same pipeline, the parameters held.\n40 milliseconds. Stripped the hum. Preserved clean voice.\nBut the warning stands: AI models simulate mastery without comprehension.\nLike the Chinese room in Peter Watts' Blindsight.\nOur job isn't to marvel at synthetic fluency.\nOur job is to walk back under the wires and test the boundary.\n",
  "[SCENE: Outro card with links]\nThat's the spirit of The Sound Above.\nAcross this 37-episode series we're going to rewatch fifteen years of conversations\nfrom the Software Craftsmanship movement.\nChicago user groups to the AI horizon.\nHow developers tackled architecture, testing, and autonomy before commercial platforms took over.\n[FREEFORM: Talk about what the archive means to you personally - why you kept those tapes,\nwhy 218 conversations is a number that still surprises you]\nFull transcripts, telemetry, and companion code are open at just3ws.com.\nIf you want to help support the independent hosting and archive preservation:\npatreon.com/whoistechcmty\nNext up: Episode 1. The first room. Sergio Pereira and Chicago Alt.NET.\nKeep your feedback loops fast. And verify your code.\n"
]